---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../consts';

/**
 * The destination for the consent bar's "Cookie Policy" link.
 *
 * Written from what the site actually loads, verified by auditing the live
 * pages: GA4 (G-JQ7BB4L4EJ) and Google Ads (AW-18419712106) through GTM. If a
 * tag is added or removed in the GTM container, the table below is what needs
 * updating with it - a policy listing cookies the site does not set, or
 * missing ones it does, is worse than none.
 */
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Cookie Policy"
  description={`Which cookies ${SITE.name} sets, what each one does, and how to change or withdraw your choice at any time.`}
>
  <div class="wrap page">
    <h1>Cookie Policy</h1>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        <strong>Nothing that tracks you runs until you say yes.</strong> Analytics and
        advertising cookies are switched off by default. They are only set if you choose
        Accept, or enable that category under Manage.
      </p>

      <h2>What a cookie is here</h2>
      <p>
        A small file a site stores in your browser. Some are needed for the site to work
        at all; others record what you read so we can measure our audience, or let
        advertisers see whether an ad led anywhere.
      </p>

      <h2>Categories</h2>

      <h3>Strictly necessary — always on</h3>
      <p>
        These are not used to identify or track you, so they do not need consent. The only
        thing {SITE.name} stores in this category is your cookie choice itself, in your
        browser's local storage under <code>critvolt.consent</code>, so we do not ask
        again on every page.
      </p>

      <h3>Analytics — off until you allow it</h3>
      <p>
        Google Analytics 4, loaded through Google Tag Manager. It tells us how many people
        read an article and which ones, which is how we decide what to write next. We do
        not use it to identify individuals.
      </p>

      <h3>Advertising — off until you allow it</h3>
      <p>
        Google Ads. Lets advertisers measure whether an ad worked and personalise what you
        are shown. Declining does not remove advertising; it means what you see is not
        personalised using these cookies.
      </p>

      <h2>The cookies themselves</h2>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Name</th>
              <th>Set by</th>
              <th>Category</th>
              <th>Purpose</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><code>critvolt.consent</code></td>
              <td>{SITE.name}</td>
              <td>Necessary</td>
              <td>Remembers your choice on this bar. Local storage, not sent anywhere.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>_ga</code></td>
              <td>Google Analytics</td>
              <td>Analytics</td>
              <td>Distinguishes one browser from another so visits are not double-counted.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>_ga_JQ7BB4L4EJ</code></td>
              <td>Google Analytics</td>
              <td>Analytics</td>
              <td>Keeps session state for this property.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>_gcl_au</code></td>
              <td>Google Ads</td>
              <td>Advertising</td>
              <td>Attributes a later action back to an ad you clicked.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="note">
        Google may set further cookies once advertising is allowed. Their current list is
        maintained at <a
          href="https://business.safety.google/adscookies/"
          rel="noopener nofollow"
          target="_blank">business.safety.google/adscookies</a
        >.
      </p>

      <h2>Changing your mind</h2>
      <p>
        <a href="#" data-cc-reopen>Reopen your cookie choices</a> at any time. Withdrawing
        consent is as easy as giving it, and takes effect immediately — though cookies
        already set are removed by your browser, not by us. You can clear them for this
        site in your browser's settings.
      </p>

      <h2>Do Not Track and browser controls</h2>
      <p>
        Every browser can block or delete cookies, and blocking the ones above will not
        break anything on this site. If you block them at the browser level, our bar has
        nothing left to switch on.
      </p>

      <h2>Questions</h2>
      <p>
        See the <a href="/affiliate-disclosure/">Affiliate Disclosure</a> for how the site
        earns money, and <a href="/about/">About</a> for who runs it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .table-wrap {
    overflow-x: auto;
    margin: 1.2rem 0;
  }
  table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    min-width: 640px;
  }
  th,
  td {
    text-align: left;
    padding: 10px 12px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-soft);
    vertical-align: top;
  }
  th {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    border-bottom-color: var(--border);
  }
  td code {
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .note {
    font-size: 0.86rem;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
</style>
